+Formal safety proof & self‑healing controller scaffold
+===============================================
+
+What this provides
+- A TLA+ sketch (specs/safety/safety_spec.tla) describing high‑level safety invariants.
+- A Z3-based bounded model checker (tools/formal/z3_bmc.py) that unrolls the state machine up to a bounded depth and searches for counterexamples to safety invariants.
+- A runtime safety monitor (ops/autonomy/safety_monitor.py) that enforces key preconditions for autonomous actions and records evidence when actions are allowed/blocked.
+- A conservative self‑healing controller (controllers/self_healing_controller.py) that consults the safety monitor, performs actions, probes post‑conditions, and rolls back / engages safe_mode on failures.
+- A proof evidence builder (ops/autonomy/proof_evidence_builder.py) to collect Z3 traces, sign artifacts and upload to evidence S3 + Rekor (best effort).
+- CI workflow (.github/workflows/formal_verification.yml) to run the bounded model checker and publish artifacts for auditors.
+
+How to use
+1. Run a bounded check locally:
+   python3 tools/formal/z3_bmc.py --depth 6
+   - If Z3 returns "No counterexample found up to depth N", you have bounded assurance for the modeled invariants.
+
+2. Integrate runtime safety_monitor before any autonomous action:
+   - Call safety_monitor.check_and_allow(action, metadata) and only proceed when True.
+   - The monitor will record signed manifests and upload them to evidence bucket if configured.
+
+3. Produce proof bundle for auditors:
+   - Run: python3 ops/autonomy/proof_evidence_builder.py
+   - Deliver the produced artifacts and Rekor UUIDs to auditing teams.
+
+Limitations & next steps
+- The Z3 bounded model check gives bounded guarantees (up to depth K). For unbounded proofs use theorem provers or TLA+ with induction (Apalache/TLC) and supply human proofs.
+- The TLA+ spec is a human‑readable sketch — use Apalache/TLC to run model checking; produce invariants and properties for independent auditors.
+- The self‑healing controller and coordinator code are scaffolds and must be tested under load and audited for correctness.
+- For regulator acceptance you will need: (A) formal artifacts (Z3/TLA+ runs, proof obligations, discharge logs), (B) reproducible scripts that re-run checks and produce signed artifacts, (C) auditor-attested proof documents.
+
+Auditor checklist for formal proofs
+- Reproduce the Z3 BMC run and confirm counterexamples (if any) are handled.
+- Review TLA+ spec and validate property statements.
+- Validate runtime monitor hooks and evidence manifests are generated/signed/uploaded.
+- Confirm self‑healer rollback semantics preserve safety invariants and documentation maps proofs to controller code.
+
